--- body ---
## Context

`fetch.py` routes every ISBN to LibGen whatever `--source` says. LibGen is a shadow library behind
the opt-in `shadow` extra, so a default install cannot fetch any book.

The original plan, a DOAB/OAPEN source, fails its premise: both REST search endpoints answer
403 "You address is not allowed to access this API" (padme, 2026-10-29), and OAI-PMH has no ISBN
filter. Author ruling, 2026-10-29: reshape. Crossref `works?filter=isbn:<isbn>` returns the DOIs
registered for a book (OAPEN/DOAB titles are mostly DOI-registered), and the existing open DOI
sources (Unpaywall, HAL, later OpenAlex) already find their open-access PDFs.

## Actions
1. An ISBN resolver under 0008's contract: Crossref `works?filter=isbn:<isbn>` -> candidate DOIs,
   book-level records (`type` book, monograph, edited-book, reference-book) preferred over chapters.
   Check on real ISBNs which `type` values come back before fixing the preference order.
2. ISBN requests in the default cascade: resolve to a DOI, then run the open DOI sources on it; the
   file is named from the book metadata (0011). No DOI found or no open copy -> `failed` with a reason.
3. ISBN dispatch goes through `ACCEPTS` like every other kind: drop the LibGen special case. LibGen
   runs only when selected explicitly, consistent with the open-plus-institutional default (0008 log).
4. README: books are fetched from open sources via their DOI by default; LibGen opt-in.
5. Mock all network in tests; one `network`-marked test on a known open-access book ISBN.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] An open-access book is fetched by ISBN on a default install (no extras)
- [ ] No ISBN special case left in the CLI dispatch
- [ ] Conformance suite (0009) covers the ISBN resolution path
